  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Sunday)\nIssued at 106 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nRidging aloft over the Plains, downstream from an upper trough\nover the western CONUS, will continue to support near record\nwarmth on Monday. A tighter gradient over the region between lee\ntroughing to our west and a surface high along the Gulf Coast will\ncontinue to bring gusty south to southwest winds. The winds and\nwarm temps will yield higher spread indices across NE OK and far\nNW AR, so it is likely that another fire danger statement will be\nneeded. Winds will not be quite as strong on Tuesday and humidity\nwill be on the increase, limiting the fire weather danger some.\n\nThe models are in better agreement today with the evolution of the\nupper air pattern this coming week, increasing confidence in the\nforecast details. An upper trough over the southwest CONUS is \nexpected to eject into the central Plains by Thursday, eventually \nphasing with a strong upper trough in the northern stream diving \nsoutheast over the Great Lakes.\n\nStorms are expected to develop by late Tuesday along focusing\nboundaries to our north and west with chances increasing into our\narea Tuesday night into Wednesday as the effective boundary gets\nforced down into our region. With warm and humid air in place over\nthe area ahead of the boundary, some severe weather will be\npossible. Models suggest another round of rain and storms is \nexpected Wednesday night into Thursday as lift increases ahead of \nthe ejecting southern stream wave and advancing polar front. Thus,\nmultiple rounds of rain and storms are expected within a plume of\nunseasonably high PWATs that stretch from Mexico north into the\nregion. EPS/GEFS mean total QPFs are in the 1.5 to 2 inch range,\nwith deterministic guidance showing potential for locally heavy\ntotals in the 5 to 6 inch range through Thursday. The majority of\nthis QPF is focused across northeast OK, possibly bleeding into\nfar northwest AR. The widespread moderate to locally heavy rains\ncould lead to some river flooding, but the bigger threat at this\npoint appears to be localized flash flooding. There will have been\nabout 2 weeks since the last rainfall, with an extended drought\nbefore that from the summer. So it is uncertain as to how much of\nthe rainfall will become runoff. Nevertheless, the localized heavy\nrain potential may warrant a Flood Watch in the coming days.\n\nRain/storm chances drop off Thursday night as the polar cold front\nadvances south and east across the region, with a dry forecast by\nFriday. A much cooler and more seasonable airmass will move into\nthe region behind the front, dropping forecast high temps below\naverage for this time of year Friday and Saturday (sort of hard to\nbelieve with how warm it has been). Overnight lows over the\nweekend will drop into the 40s with maybe even some upper 30s in\nthe sheltered valley locations. There will be a true fall-like \nfeel to the air, for a change.\n\nLacy",
